sections.body:
Before reaching for a framework, it's worth being honest about what a 'test' without one actually looks like: a script that calls your function, prints the result, and relies on a human reading the output and deciding whether it looks right. That works for exactly as long as a project has one person, running tests manually, immediately after writing the code they're testing -- and it falls apart the moment any of those conditions stops holding. Nobody reliably re-reads forty lines of printed output after every change to notice that line 23 quietly started printing the wrong thing. A test framework like Jest (or Node's own built-in node:test runner, which uses a very similar API) replaces 'a human decides if the output looks right' with 'a specific expression is compared against a specific expected value, and the framework itself decides pass or fail, every single time, without anyone reading anything.'

```javascript
// Without a framework: you have to notice this is wrong
console.log(add(2, 3)); // prints 5 -- looks right, but who's checking?

// With Jest: the framework checks for you, every run, forever
test('adds two numbers', () => {
  expect(add(2, 3)).toBe(5);
});
```

This distinction -- assertion versus observation -- is the entire reason automated tests catch regressions that manual testing doesn't: a test doesn't get bored, doesn't skim, and doesn't forget to check the edge case it checked yesterday.

Jest's two structural building blocks are describe (a named group of related tests) and it or test (a single test case; they're exact synonyms -- it reads more naturally as a sentence, test reads more like an instruction, pick one convention per project and stay consistent). Nesting describe blocks groups tests by the unit of code they exercise, and produces readable, hierarchical output when a suite runs:

```javascript
function divide(a, b) {
  if (b === 0) throw new Error('Cannot divide by zero');
  return a / b;
}

describe('divide', () => {
  it('divides two positive numbers', () => {
    expect(divide(10, 2)).toBe(5);
  });

  it('throws when dividing by zero', () => {
    expect(() => divide(10, 0)).toThrow('Cannot divide by zero');
  });

  describe('with negative numbers', () => {
    it('divides a negative by a positive', () => {
      expect(divide(-10, 2)).toBe(-5);
    });
  });
});
```

Crucially, each it block runs as its own isolated unit -- Jest catches any thrown error or failed expect inside one it block, marks that specific test as failed, and moves on to run every other it block regardless. This is a genuinely important property: one broken test doesn't hide or block the results of the nine unrelated tests around it, which is exactly the opposite of what a script full of sequential console.log calls gives you, where an uncaught exception on line 10 stops the whole script from ever reaching line 40. describe blocks themselves don't contain assertions directly -- they're purely organizational, and Jest reports failures scoped by which describe/it path they occurred in, so a large suite's output tells you immediately not just 'something broke' but 'divide > with negative numbers > divides a negative by a positive broke,' which matters enormously once a suite has hundreds of tests.

A **unit test** exercises one function or module in isolation, with its dependencies either genuinely absent or replaced by mocks (covered in the next concept) -- as opposed to an **integration test**, which exercises multiple real pieces working together (a route handler that really talks to a real, if temporary, database). The practical reason isolation matters day to day is test independence: any given test should pass or fail based only on the code it's actually testing, not on which other tests happened to run before it, or in what order.

A common way independence breaks: a shared, mutable variable that one test's setup or assertions modify, which then leaks into a differently-ordered or differently-filtered test run:

```javascript
let cart = [];

describe('shopping cart', () => {
  it('starts empty', () => {
    expect(cart).toHaveLength(0);   // passes if run first, fails if run after the next test
  });

  it('adds an item', () => {
    cart.push('apple');
    expect(cart).toHaveLength(1);
  });
});
```

Run top to bottom, this looks fine -- but Jest does not guarantee tests run in file order once you introduce test filtering, parallel workers, or a different runner configuration, and even within one file, the 'starts empty' test is silently depending on 'adds an item' never having run yet, which is a fragile, accidental coupling rather than a deliberate one. beforeEach(fn) (run before every it in its enclosing describe) and afterEach(fn) (run after every one) exist specifically to reset shared state deterministically between tests:

```javascript
let cart;

beforeEach(() => {
  cart = [];   // fresh, empty array before EVERY test, regardless of order
});

it('starts empty', () => {
  expect(cart).toHaveLength(0);
});

it('adds an item', () => {
  cart.push('apple');
  expect(cart).toHaveLength(1);
});
```

Now each test gets a guaranteed-fresh cart, and the two tests could run in either order, or even in separate parallel workers, without affecting each other's outcome. beforeAll/afterAll are the once-per-describe-block equivalents, useful for genuinely expensive setup (like opening a database connection) that's safe to share read-only across tests, but reach for beforeEach by default -- it's the safer choice whenever there's any doubt about whether state is being mutated.

examples.code:
// stringUtils.js
function slugify(text) {
  return text.trim().toLowerCase().replace(/\s+/g, '-');
}

module.exports = { slugify };

// stringUtils.test.js
const { slugify } = require('./stringUtils');

describe('slugify', () => {
  it('lowercases and hyphenates spaces', () => {
    expect(slugify('Hello World')).toBe('hello-world');
  });

  it('trims leading and trailing whitespace', () => {
    expect(slugify('  Hello World  ')).toBe('hello-world');
  });

  it('collapses multiple internal spaces into one hyphen', () => {
    expect(slugify('Hello    World')).toBe('hello-world');
  });
});

function createUser(name) {
  return { name, roles: ['member'], createdAt: null };
}

test('creates a user with default role', () => {
  const user = createUser('Amy');

  // WRONG matcher: fails even though the object 'looks' correct
  // expect(user).toBe({ name: 'Amy', roles: ['member'], createdAt: null });

  // RIGHT matcher: structural comparison
  expect(user).toEqual({ name: 'Amy', roles: ['member'], createdAt: null });
});

