jest.fn() creates a mock function -- a function that does nothing by default (returns undefined), but records every call made to it: what arguments each call received, how many times it was called, and in what order relative to other mock calls. You can configure what it returns, and afterward, assert on how it was used:

```javascript
const sendEmail = jest.fn();
sendEmail.mockReturnValue('queued');

const result = sendEmail('amy@example.com', 'Welcome!');

expect(result).toBe('queued');
expect(sendEmail).toHaveBeenCalledWith('amy@example.com', 'Welcome!');
expect(sendEmail).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(1);
```

This is the key capability real functions don't give you for free: you're not just checking 'did this eventually produce the right output,' you're checking 'did my code under test actually call this dependency, with these exact arguments, this many times' -- which matters enormously for functions whose entire job is to trigger a side effect (send an email, write a log, call an API) rather than return a value you can inspect directly. For asynchronous dependencies, mockResolvedValue(value) and mockRejectedValue(error) configure a mock to return a Promise that resolves or rejects, which is what you'd use to stand in for an async database call or API request.

jest.fn() handles one function passed explicitly as an argument, but a lot of real dependencies are pulled in via require/import inside the module under test, not passed in as a parameter -- a database client, an HTTP library, another internal module. jest.mock('./path/to/module') tells Jest to intercept every require/import of that specific module path, inside the current test file, and substitute an automatically generated mocked version (every exported function replaced with a jest.fn()) instead of the real thing:

```javascript
// db.js
function getUser(id) {
  // in reality: a real database query
}
module.exports = { getUser };

// userService.js
const db = require('./db');

async function getUserDisplayName(id) {
  const user = await db.getUser(id);
  if (!user) return 'Unknown User';
  return user.name;
}
module.exports = { getUserDisplayName };

// userService.test.js
jest.mock('./db');
const db = require('./db');
const { getUserDisplayName } = require('./userService');

test('returns the user\'s name when found', async () => {
  db.getUser.mockResolvedValue({ id: 1, name: 'Amy' });
  const name = await getUserDisplayName(1);
  expect(name).toBe('Amy');
});

test('returns a fallback when the user does not exist', async () => {
  db.getUser.mockResolvedValue(null);
  const name = await getUserDisplayName(1);
  expect(name).toBe('Unknown User');
});
```

Notice what this buys you: the second test simulates 'the database found nothing' -- a scenario that might be awkward to reliably reproduce against a real database (you'd have to guarantee a specific id genuinely doesn't exist) -- by simply telling the mock what to return. getUserDisplayName's actual logic (the if/else branching on whether a user was found) is exercised for real; only db.getUser's actual implementation is faked. This is the core discipline of a good unit test: mock the boundary (the dependency), keep the logic under test real.

examples.code:
function processOrder(order, onComplete) {
  const total = order.items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + item.price, 0);
  onComplete(total);
  return total;
}

test('calls onComplete with the computed total', () => {
  const onComplete = jest.fn();
  const order = { items: [{ price: 10 }, { price: 25 }] };

  processOrder(order, onComplete);

  expect(onComplete).toHaveBeenCalledWith(35);
  expect(onComplete).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(1);
});

// paymentGateway.js
async function charge(cardToken, amountCents) {
  // in reality: a real network call to a payment provider
}
module.exports = { charge };

// checkout.js
const paymentGateway = require('./paymentGateway');

async function completeCheckout(cardToken, cart) {
  const total = cart.reduce((sum, item) => sum + item.priceCents, 0);
  const result = await paymentGateway.charge(cardToken, total);
  if (result.status !== 'approved') {
    throw new Error('Payment declined');
  }
  return { total, status: result.status };
}
module.exports = { completeCheckout };

// checkout.test.js
jest.mock('./paymentGateway');
const paymentGateway = require('./paymentGateway');
const { completeCheckout } = require('./checkout');

test('throws when the payment is declined', async () => {
  paymentGateway.charge.mockResolvedValue({ status: 'declined' });
  const cart = [{ priceCents: 500 }];

  await expect(completeCheckout('tok_123', cart)).rejects.toThrow('Payment declined');
});

