sections.body:
supertest wraps an Express app object and lets you write requests against it using a fluent, chainable API, then assert on the actual response that comes back -- status code, headers, body -- exactly as a real HTTP client would see it:

```javascript
const request = require('supertest');
const app = require('./app');   // your Express app, NOT already listening

test('GET /health returns 200 and status ok', async () => {
  const response = await request(app).get('/health');

  expect(response.status).toBe(200);
  expect(response.body).toEqual({ status: 'ok' });
});
```

A detail that trips people up coming from manual API testing with a tool like curl or Postman: you do **not** call app.listen() anywhere in this test, and there is no real port being bound. supertest takes the Express app object directly and, under the hood, starts an ephemeral, random-port HTTP server for just the duration of that one request, then tears it down -- your app's routing and middleware pipeline runs for real (this is genuinely an HTTP request, with real headers and a real response cycle, not a shortcut that skips Express's routing), but you're spared the ceremony of managing a real server process, picking a fixed port that might conflict with something else already running, or remembering to shut it down after each test. This is precisely why supertest is described as testing 'the app,' not 'a server' -- the distinction matters because it's what makes these tests fast and safe to run many times in parallel without port collisions.

A route is not fully tested by only checking its happy path. Real routes need to handle malformed input, missing resources, and unauthorized requests, and each of those should be asserted explicitly, with the exact status code and body shape a real client would need to branch on:

```javascript
const request = require('supertest');
const app = require('./app');

describe('POST /users', () => {
  it('creates a user and returns 201 with the created resource', async () => {
    const response = await request(app)
      .post('/users')
      .send({ name: 'Amy', email: 'amy@example.com' });

    expect(response.status).toBe(201);
    expect(response.body).toMatchObject({ name: 'Amy', email: 'amy@example.com' });
    expect(response.body.id).toBeDefined();
  });

  it('returns 400 when email is missing', async () => {
    const response = await request(app)
      .post('/users')
      .send({ name: 'Amy' });

    expect(response.status).toBe(400);
    expect(response.body.error).toMatch(/email/i);
  });
});

describe('GET /users/:id', () => {
  it('returns 404 for a user that does not exist', async () => {
    const response = await request(app).get('/users/999999');
    expect(response.status).toBe(404);
  });
});
```

.send(body) attaches a JSON request body (supertest sets the Content-Type header for you when given a plain object) -- this is exactly what verifies that a body-parsing middleware like express.json() is actually registered and working, something no unit test of an isolated handler function would ever exercise, since a unit test calling the handler function directly would just pass in a plain JavaScript object, bypassing the whole parse-the-raw-request-stream step entirely. toMatchObject checks that the response body contains at least the given key/value pairs, without requiring an exact full match -- useful here because the real id is generated by the server and can't be known in advance by the test.

examples.code:
// app.js
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
app.use(express.json());

const users = [{ id: 1, name: 'Amy' }];

app.get('/users/:id', (req, res) => {
  const user = users.find(u => u.id === Number(req.params.id));
  if (!user) return res.status(404).json({ error: 'User not found' });
  res.json(user);
});

module.exports = app;   // NOTE: no app.listen() here -- that belongs in a separate entry file

// app.test.js
const request = require('supertest');
const app = require('./app');

describe('GET /users/:id', () => {
  it('returns the user when found', async () => {
    const response = await request(app).get('/users/1');
    expect(response.status).toBe(200);
    expect(response.body).toEqual({ id: 1, name: 'Amy' });
  });

  it('returns 404 when the user does not exist', async () => {
    const response = await request(app).get('/users/999');
    expect(response.status).toBe(404);
    expect(response.body).toEqual({ error: 'User not found' });
  });
});

const request = require('supertest');
const app = require('./app');

test('POST /echo returns the JSON body that was sent', async () => {
  const response = await request(app)
    .post('/echo')
    .send({ message: 'hello' })
    .set('Content-Type', 'application/json');

  expect(response.status).toBe(200);
  expect(response.body).toEqual({ youSent: { message: 'hello' } });
});

